Square Maxwell scattering matrix on an enclosing sphere
=======================================================

Run
  From the repository root in MATLAB:

    run('startup.m')
    result = test_ellipsoid_scattering(16,20);

  The arguments are the ellipsoid patch order and the sphere polar order.
  Use (8,16) for a faster, lower-accuracy check. Results, including Scat, are
  saved to test/results/ellipsoid_p<order>_q<proxy_order>.mat.

Geometry and conventions
  A perfectly conducting ellipsoid has semiaxes [0.5,0.4,0.3]. With k=2*pi
  its largest diameter is one wavelength. The enclosing sphere has radius 1.
  Units are normalized to epsilon=mu=1 with time dependence exp(-i*omega*t).
  For physical H in other units, use impedance-scaled H in this interface.

  At each of m sphere nodes, store four tangential field components:

    d = [Eu_1; Ev_1; Hu_1; Hv_1; Eu_2; Ev_2; Hu_2; Hv_2; ...].

  Both input and output use exactly the same nodes and unit tangent frames.
  Scat is therefore 4*m by 4*m. These are field values, without quadrature
  weights; the evaluation matrices include the source weights. At order 20,
  m=800 and Scat is 3200 by 3200 (about 164 MB in complex double precision).

Construction
  A is the existing corrected NRCCIE operator on the actual ellipsoid.
  R maps incident E,H samples there to the PEC right hand side.
  L extracts tangential E,H on the sphere.
  Q is the Stratton-Chu matrix from sphere traces to separated target fields.
  K evaluates the physical NRCCIE density on the sphere.

    B = R*(-Q(sphere -> ellipsoid))
    C = L*K(ellipsoid -> sphere)
    Scat = C*(A\B)

  The minus sign in B is essential: the outward-oriented Green representation
  reconstructs a regular incident field with a minus sign inside the sphere.
  Outside, Q reconstructs an outgoing field with a plus sign.

  Only the physical NRCCIE is solved. No first-kind integral equation on the
  sphere is solved. The demo forms/factors A densely because this is a small
  ellipsoid; the interface does not require dense A for a larger geometry.

Apply to a new incident field
  Let Einc,Hinc be 3-by-m arrays sampled at result.proxy.r, with sources
  outside the enclosing sphere. Then:

    P = result.proxy;
    L = maxwell_trace_matrix(P);
    din = L*reshape([Einc;Hinc],[],1);
    dout = result.Scat*din;
    T.r = targets;                         % 3-by-nt, outside the sphere
    Q = stratton_chu_matrix(P,T,result.zk);
    EH = reshape(Q*dout,6,[]);
    Esc = EH(1:3,:);
    Hsc = EH(4:6,:);

  This yields scattered fields. Add the original incident fields at targets
  to obtain total fields. Targets need not lie on a sphere. The current
  quadrature is intended for separated targets; targets very close to the
  proxy require finer or special quadrature.

  Four sampled trace components are redundant coordinates for physically
  compatible Maxwell fields. Scat is square but need not be invertible.
  It maps incident traces to scattered traces; it is not a change of basis.

Stratton-Chu implementation
  Set J=n x H and M=-n x E on the sphere. With G=exp(ik*r)/(4*pi*r),

    E = ik*(S J + grad div S J/k^2) - curl S M,
    H = curl S J + ik*(S M + grad div S M/k^2).

  stratton_chu_matrix evaluates these kernels directly, including the Hessian
  of G. No numerical surface differentiation of sampled fields is needed.
  This is the tangential-current form of the Maxwell Green representation.

Validation
  Three new plane waves and one exterior electric dipole are checked. None
  serves as a training field. Targets sample 80 directions at each radius
  2, 4, and 8 wavelengths. Reported relative Euclidean errors distinguish:

    incoming      sphere trace -> incident field on physical ellipsoid
    trace         Scat output vs direct NRCCIE scattered sphere trace
    target        Scat + Stratton-Chu vs direct NRCCIE target evaluation
    stratton_chu  direct scattered sphere trace -> target vs direct BIE
    bie_residual  residual of the physical density solve

  An analytic interior dipole additionally checks the outward Green identity
  independently of the BIE. Physical mesh refinement and the separate
  check_ellipsoid_reference function assess physical discretization error.
  Increasing proxy order alone does not eliminate physical discretization
  error, including discrete current/charge compatibility error.

Measured results (2026-10-12)
  Maxima over the four incident fields, using the direct physical BIE field
  as the target comparison:

    physical order  sphere order    incoming       trace          target
          8              16         4.18e-9        2.18e-9        6.05e-4
         12              20         1.57e-11       7.65e-12       5.58e-6
         16              20         1.60e-11       2.75e-12       1.60e-7

  The last construction used 1734 physical nodes (5202 NRCCIE unknowns),
  took 45.4 seconds with one MATLAB computation thread, and had a maximum
  relative BIE residual of 6.1e-15. The analytic outgoing-dipole Green
  identity error was 5.7e-12. See test/results/ellipsoid_validation.png.

  check_ellipsoid_reference([],16) independently solved the +x incident
  plane wave using em3d.pec.solver/eval with oversampled quadrature. GMRES
  took 42 iterations with residual 6.48e-12. Compared with that reference,
  Scat plus Stratton-Chu had relative E/H differences of 3.35e-7/3.25e-7.
  These comparisons do not establish a rigorous continuous-solution error
  bound. An additional order-20 reference attempt crashed before producing
  its result, so no order-20 convergence claim is made.

  On this Mac, the three matrix-construction runs saved their results and
  then crashed in MATLAB's thread cleanup at process exit. This reproduces the pre-existing
  MEX environment issue; it is not a clean process exit. A separate MATLAB
  process loaded all saved results, passed geometry/frame/error assertions,
  checked the source files, rendered the figure, and exited normally.

Files
  test_ellipsoid_scattering.m   complete construction and new-field tests
  maxwell_sphere.m              sphere quadrature and tangent frames
  maxwell_trace_matrix.m        Cartesian E,H -> four tangential components
  nrccie_rhs_matrix.m           Cartesian incident E,H -> physical BIE RHS
  stratton_chu_matrix.m         sphere trace -> Cartesian E,H at targets
  check_ellipsoid_reference.m   independent library solver/evaluator check

Shared incoming/outgoing skeleton points
  After constructing the full matrix, run:

    compressed = test_ellipsoid_shared_skeleton(1e-8);

  This separate test loads ellipsoid_p16_q20.mat. It makes incoming trace
  samples from 384 plane-wave directions (both transverse polarizations) and
  96 exterior source positions at radius 2.5 (three electric and three
  magnetic dipoles each). Outgoing samples are the corresponding scattered
  responses. Each incoming/outgoing column is normalized in the sphere
  quadrature norm before forming the joint sample matrix [Fin,Fout].

  maxwell_shared_row_id applies block-pivoted QR to that matrix's adjoint.
  Each pivot selects a complete sphere point with all four field components.
  A least-squares interpolation fit gives T with identity rows at the selected
  DOFs I. Both incoming and outgoing traces satisfy d approximately T*d(I).
  The least-squares fit constructs interpolation coefficients; no physical
  first-kind boundary-integral equation is involved.

    Scat_small = Scat_full(I,:)*T;
    dout_I = Scat_small*din_I;
    dout = T*dout_I;
    field = (Q*T)*dout_I;

  Scat_small maps actual incoming field values on the selected points to
  scattered field values on exactly those same points. Its size is 4*r by
  4*r for r selected points. The skeleton values do not serve as quadrature
  source strengths on a sparsely sampled sphere: the target map uses Q*T,
  which retains the original quadrature and the outgoing reconstruction.

  To apply a saved compressed result to new incident E,H samples at its
  skeleton.r (3-by-r arrays):

    din_I = maxwell_trace_matrix(compressed.skeleton)*reshape([Einc;Hinc],[],1);
    dout_I = compressed.Scat*din_I;
    Q = stratton_chu_matrix(compressed.proxy,targets,compressed.zk);
    EH = reshape((Q*compressed.interpolation)*dout_I,6,[]);

  Here targets is a struct with targets.r. For the saved test targets,
  compressed.target_matrix already stores Q*T. No incident samples at the
  discarded sphere nodes are needed during application.

  The prototype reuses the full scattering matrix to isolate compression
  error. The algebra also permits direct construction C(I,:)*(A\(B*T)),
  which would avoid forming Scat_full; that construction is not implemented
  in this compression test.

Measured shared-skeleton results (2026-10-12)
  At training ID tolerance 1e-8, the joint selection retained 310 of 800
  sphere points, yielding a 1240-by-1240 scattering matrix. The separately
  stored interpolation matrix is 3200-by-1240. Joint training residual was
  4.83e-9 and the largest interpolation entry was 2.01.

  Checks used 268 held-out fields: the original four BIE-tested fields,
  64 new directions with both linear polarizations, their 64 circular
  combinations, and 72 electric/magnetic dipoles at new positions with
  radii 2.5, 3, and 4. The circular fields test complex-valued application
  but do not increase the span of the held-out plane-wave space.

    incoming trace reconstruction              1.29e-7
    outgoing trace reconstruction              1.10e-8
    complete scattering trace vs full matrix   1.11e-7
    added target error from compression         1.11e-7

  These are maximum relative Euclidean errors across the held-out fields.
  The original four comparisons against direct BIE evaluation remained
  below 1.60e-7 with compression. The training tolerance controls the joint
  sample residual; it is not an all-fields error bound. Sources much closer
  to the proxy require a richer training space and new validation.

  Results and the common-node/error plot are saved as
    test/results/ellipsoid_shared_skeleton_1em08.mat
    test/results/ellipsoid_shared_skeleton_1em08.png

  This computation uses saved data and MATLAB kernels without native BIE
  calls; the initial shared-skeleton run completed with a normal exit.

Direct joint compression of B and C, without a full scattering matrix
  Run from the repository root:

    run('startup.m')
    compressed = test_ellipsoid_bc_skeleton(1e-6,16,20);

  test_ellipsoid_bc_skeleton constructs the compressed response directly
  from the two kernel maps. It does not load an existing scattering matrix.
  Its ID uses the normalized joint matrix

    F = [B'/norm(B,'fro'), C/norm(C,'fro')].

  A common block row ID gives F approximately U*F(I,:), where I contains
  all four components of each selected sphere point. Consequently,

    B approximately B(:,I)*U'
    C approximately U*C(I,:).

  Once I is selected, the only response matrix constructed is

    Scat_small = C(I,:)*(A\B(:,I)).

  This needs one factorization of the physical BIE and 4*r right-hand sides
  for r sphere skeleton points. Selection itself needs no BIE solutions.
  Direct solves for individual test fields provide the validation reference;
  the full sphere-to-sphere scattering matrix is never formed, even there.

  The compressed interface is different from the sampled-field interface:

    a = U'*din;                  incoming projection coefficients
    b = Scat_small*a;            outgoing field samples at selected nodes
    dout = U*b;                  complete outgoing sphere trace
    field = (Q*U)*b;             Stratton-Chu evaluation at targets

  Both sides use the same geometric point indices and have size 4*r.
  Incoming a combines the full sphere data; it is not din(I). The saved
  input_projection and output_interpolation matrices make this distinction
  explicit. A pure column ID of B does not provide a pointwise interpolant
  for physically compatible incoming traces.

  If actual incoming field samples at the selected points are required,
  add a regular incident-field basis Fin and do a joint row ID of [Fin,C].
  Its lift V gives the sampled-field map C(I,:)*(A\(B*V)). That route also
  avoids forming a full scattering matrix but uses extra incident-space
  information. The earlier shared-skeleton test demonstrates this type of
  sampled-field interface with outgoing response samples.

  The ellipsoid prototype still assembles dense A, B, and C. Avoiding full
  B/C storage for a large physical mesh requires sampled or blockwise
  kernel access and a scalable physical solver; this test addresses the
  full-scattering-matrix dependency specifically.

Measured direct B/C results (2026-10-12)
  All 48 validation fields were independent of the ID: 32 plane waves with
  different directions/polarizations, four complex-polarization combinations,
  and 12 electric/magnetic dipoles outside the sphere. Targets used 80
  directions at each radius 2, 4, and 8. No full Scat was formed for either
  construction or validation.

    ID tolerance  points   square matrix   added target error  vs direct BIE
       1e-8         492     1968 x 1968         1.46e-10          3.53e-7
       1e-6         323     1292 x 1292         2.93e-8           3.53e-7

  Errors are the largest relative Euclidean errors over the test fields.
  The uncompressed per-field Green-chain reference also differed from direct
  physical BIE evaluation by 3.53e-7. The default tolerance is therefore 1e-6
  for this example. At that tolerance, the B/C operator residuals were
  4.82e-7/4.93e-7, incoming RHS compression error 2.71e-7, and outgoing trace
  interpolation error 1.50e-7. Selection took 75.0 s; physical quadrature and
  factorization took 17.0 s; 1292 selected RHS solves took 1.6 s (four threads).

  Saved results:
    test/results/ellipsoid_bc_skeleton_1em06.mat
    test/results/ellipsoid_bc_skeleton_1em08.mat

  Application to a new incident trace din on all 800 proxy nodes:

    a = compressed.input_projection*din;
    b = compressed.Scat*a;
    EH = reshape(compressed.target_matrix*b,6,[]);

  target_matrix applies to the saved targets. For new separated targets use
  stratton_chu_matrix(compressed.proxy,targets,compressed.zk) multiplied by
  compressed.output_interpolation. The interpolation/projection maps occupy
  additional storage beyond the small square matrix.

  Both native runs saved results and passed their numerical assertions before
  the existing MATLAB/MEX teardown crash. Saved results are checked separately
  without native BIE calls; process-exit failure is not counted as a clean run.

Nested sphere interfaces: compressed small body coupled to a physical BIE
  Run from the repository root:

    run('startup.m')
    result = test_nested_ellipsoid_scattering(12,20,1e-6);

  Arguments are physical patch order, outer sphere polar order, and joint-ID
  tolerance. The small sphere uses polar order max(12,nproxy-4). The test
  uses k=2*pi and two physically disjoint PEC ellipsoids:

    small: semiaxes [0.12,0.10,0.08], center [0,0,0]
    large: semiaxes [0.35,0.28,0.22], center [0.90,0,0]
    small sphere: radius 0.30, center [0,0,0]
    outer sphere: radius 1.20, center [0.45,0,0]

  The large body lies outside the small sphere. The outer sphere contains
  both physical bodies and the entire small sphere. Only the interfaces are
  nested: the physical bodies do not overlap. The outer scattering matrix
  represents the coupled pair, including repeated reflections.

  First build the small body's interface from its physical matrix A_s:

    S_s = C_s(I_s,:)*(A_s\B_s(:,I_s))
    a_s = U_s'*din_s
    b_s = S_s*a_s
    dout_s = U_s*b_s.

  The remaining construction accesses the small component only through its
  sphere geometry, U_s, and S_s. Its physical density is recovered later only
  for diagnostic comparisons. With q_d denoting the large body's density,
  define the two sphere/physical coupling maps:

    G_sd = U_s'*L_s*K(large -> small sphere)
    G_ds = R_d*Q(small sphere -> large)*U_s.

  K evaluates NRCCIE densities, Q is the outward Stratton-Chu representation,
  L extracts the four tangential traces, and R forms the incident-field RHS.
  The small sphere's exterior formula applies on the large physical body.
  Its response is coupled to the large physical BIE through

    H = [A_d,       -G_ds;
         -S_s*G_sd, identity]
    H*[q_d;b_s] = [R_d*EHinc_d; S_s*U_s'*din_s].

  The full H solve includes feedback in both directions. No propagation of
  an outgoing outer-sphere field into its interior is used for this coupling.

  For the outer interface, define the incoming and outgoing maps

    B_o = [-R_d*Q(outer -> large);
           -S_s*U_s'*L_s*Q(outer -> small sphere)]
    C_o = [L_o*K(large -> outer), L_o*Q(small sphere -> outer)*U_s].

  The minus signs in B_o reconstruct a regular incident field inside the
  outward-oriented outer sphere. Joint ID selects the outer DOFs I_o, then

    S_o = C_o(I_o,:)*(H\B_o(:,I_o)).

  Both S_s and S_o are square, with four DOFs per retained sphere point. No
  full sphere-to-sphere scattering matrix is constructed at either stage.
  The trace ordering is [Eu,Ev,Hu,Hv] per node in unit tangent frames, without
  weights; source quadrature weights are included in the evaluation maps.
  Incoming coefficients U'*din combine the full sphere trace and are not
  incident samples din(I). Outgoing coefficients are the retained field
  samples, and U reconstructs the complete outgoing trace. For a new outer
  incident trace din and targets outside result.outer.proxy:

    a = result.outer.input_projection*din;
    b = result.outer.Scat*a;
    Q = stratton_chu_matrix(result.outer.proxy,targets,result.zk);
    EHsc = reshape(Q*(result.outer.output_interpolation*b),6,[]);

  Both interfaces use maxwell_multilevel_row_id with blocks of four rows per
  sphere point. It splits points spatially, performs deterministic local IDs,
  then merges and recompresses only child skeletons. Kernel callbacks stream
  the normalized joint rows [B'/norm(B,'fro'),C/norm(C,'fro')]; normalization
  and operator-error checks are also streamed. No randomized sketch or
  preassembled global B/C input is required. Callbacks assemble candidate
  blocks only, but a root candidate block can contain all points when
  compression is weak. Full interpolation matrices are retained for this
  prototype. Physical matrices and H are dense; this is a small coupling
  verification, not a scalable dish solve. Local tol/nlevels is a heuristic,
  and actual joint and separate B/C residuals are recorded.

  Validation uses 12 new linear-polarization plane waves, two circular
  combinations, and exterior electric/magnetic dipoles. A separate full
  two-body corrected NRCCIE solve provides the same-discretization reference.
  Its cross blocks retain charge-continuity terms; the field-only expression
  -R*K is not substituted for those true NRCCIE blocks. Separate diagnostics
  quantify charge-continuity compatibility on solved densities and compare a
  field-coupled physical system with that full reference. Hybrid residuals,
  sphere traces, and exterior E/H errors distinguish the small-interface
  approximation, outer-interface approximation, and Green-representation
  error. A control omitting feedback on the small body checks that coupling
  matters. These comparisons do not establish physical mesh convergence.

  The result contains small and outer interface data, geometry, errors, timing,
  and target fields. Data are saved before the final pass assertion, so a
  failed check can also be inspected. The default output files are

    test/results/nested_ellipsoid_p12_q20_1em06.mat
    test/results/nested_ellipsoid_p12_q20_1em06.png

  The plot shows an equatorial geometry section and errors against the full
  corrected NRCCIE reference. Other arguments change the filename tag.

  Measured results (physical order 12, joint-ID tolerance 1e-6):

    outer polar order                       20             28
    small sphere points / skeletons         512 / 290      1152 / 311
    outer sphere points / skeletons         800 / 277      1568 / 301
    small scattering matrix size            1160 x 1160    1244 x 1244
    outer scattering matrix size            1108 x 1108    1204 x 1204
    hybrid system dimension                 4202           4286
    full physical system dimension          6084           6084
    max B/C relative Frobenius error         2.98e-7        2.82e-7
    hybrid residual in full NRCCIE           4.18e-7        4.18e-7
    hybrid exterior E/H error                3.75e-7        3.75e-7
    outer compressed exterior E/H error      4.64e-5        2.74e-6
    feedback-omitted exterior E/H error      5.36e-2        5.36e-2
    total time, four MATLAB threads          149.8 s        260.9 s

  Exterior errors are the worst relative 2-norm over 16 incident fields,
  using all six E/H components at 144 targets on radii 1.6, 2.4, and 4.0
  about the outer sphere center. For the refined sphere, the separate E/H
  errors are 3.68e-6 and 1.32e-6. Per-target-radius errors are 2.77e-6,
  2.70e-6, and 2.69e-6. To reproduce that run:

    result = test_nested_ellipsoid_scattering(12,28,1e-6);

  Sphere refinement removes most of the closest-target quadrature error.
  The uncompressed full-reference trace evaluated through Stratton-Chu has
  error 2.75e-6 on the refined sphere, so the remaining discrepancy cannot
  be attributed to ID alone. Physical discretization and Green-representation
  compatibility remain distinct from the recorded B/C compression errors.
  Physical patch order is held fixed; this is not a mesh-convergence study.

  The largest joint callback blocks were 356/358 points (small/outer) at
  order 20 and 408/391 points at order 28, smaller than each full sphere.
  Neither run assembled the full sphere scattering matrix before selection.

  Both native computations saved their results and passed the numerical
  assertions before a MATLAB shutdown crash. A separate MATLAB process,
  without loading the native BIE libraries, reloaded both files, checked
  dimensions, skeleton interpolation identities, recorded errors, and a new
  application of the saved outer interface; it passed and exited normally.
  The native process-exit failures are not counted as clean executions.
